  "summary": "Keio Flying Squadron 2 is Victor Entertainment's Saturn sequel, first released in Japan on 17 May 1996.",
  "body": [
    "Keio Flying Squadron 2 is Victor Entertainment's Saturn sequel, first released in Japan on 17 May 1996. Rami Nanahikari returns in a comic treasure hunt, but the adventure now centers on platforming with shooting sections woven into it. That shift brings the heroine closer to the player: running, jumping and interacting with objects occupy the foreground between the airborne passages. Animated scenes continue the series' playful presentation, making its exaggerated characters and unusual version of historical Japan central to the journey.",
    "The change of genre grew from the team's wish to show the characters more prominently through larger sprites. Platform stages give Rami a physical presence that differs from the first game's constant flight, while the shooter sections preserve a connection to that earlier adventure. Moving between the two changes the rhythm of the journey. The player alternates between reading the shape of the ground and responding to an advancing screen, with the same cast and visual humor connecting those different demands.",
    "Development began in 1994 as an enhanced Sega CD project before moving to Saturn and becoming a full sequel. The team retained two-dimensional play as three-dimensional games became more prominent. Studio Pierrot again produced the animated scenes, and Tsukasa Tawada composed the music. A European release followed in September 1996. The project shows a studio using a more powerful machine to enlarge an established illustrated world, making the characters and their animation the reason for changing how the adventure plays."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Rami steps into the foreground",
    "A platform adventure with flying passages",
    "Keeping animation at the center"
  ],
